 # Companion widget
 
-## Window and interaction model
+## What a widget is
 
-The widget is the second declarative entry in `tauri.conf.json`, not a runtime `WebviewWindowBuilder`. One config entry guarantees one native `widget` label at startup, avoids duplicate creation races, and keeps Settings, tray, and recovery looking up the same label. It starts as a transparent 56px always-on-top figure and is resized to a 520×720 panel when expanded.
+A widget is a small, purpose-built mini version of a tab that does exactly ONE job. It is not a second window that opens a whole tab. If a job needs the calendar, the editor, the bookshelf, or any other part of the full tab, that job belongs in the app, and the widget's "open in app" button jumps there.
 
-The expanded panel has no application sidebar, tab strip, or breadcrumb chrome. It renders exactly one selected workspace using the same lazy-loading component and workspace data contracts as the main app. The canonical workspace list includes Home, Logs, Write, Inbox, Map, Canvas, Novel, Script, Projects, Reader, Files, and Library. Write uses the existing `EditorPane` in companion mode so the lightweight dock does not start editor AI/voice features; Files deep-links to Library’s Files view. The selected workspace remains persisted in Settings, and handoff requests close the widget only after the main window acknowledges success.
+| Kind | Job | Opens in app |
+|---|---|---|
+| Quick note | Type a thought, Ctrl+Enter, it is appended to today's log in the same format the Logs tab writes | Logs |
+| Reader | Read the book you are on, one section at a time (book picker, prev/next, text size) | Reader |
 
-The collapsed figure is draggable and expands on click or keyboard activation. The expanded titlebar can be dragged, collapses on click-away, and exposes an explicit collapse control. Dock edge, dock offset, collapsed state, visibility, and OS-startup preference are persisted through the existing settings store.
+Only kinds listed in `src/lib/widgets/registry.ts` exist. Adding one means writing its mini component under `src/lib/components/widgets/` and adding it to the registry, the `widgetKind` setting enum, and `WIDGET_ALLOWED_COMMANDS` in `tests/widget-invariants.mjs`.
 
-## Lifecycle and startup
+## Window model
 
-Settings → General controls widget visibility, the selected canonical workspace, dock edge, dock offset, and “Launch at OS startup.” The startup control uses Tauri’s autostart plugin. The native tray keeps the Show / Hide Widget item and the main-window access item. The widget can remain available while the main window is hidden; the tray and widget controls can bring the main window back when needed.
+`WidgetApp.svelte` is only a frame: a drag bar with open-in-app and hide, around the selected mini widget. It is a normal rectangle (no collapsed bubble, no edge docking). The OS enforces the minimum size from `tauri.conf.json` (280x180); the registry caps the maximum (720x1000) and gives each kind a first-run size. The widget remembers its last position and size (`widgetGeometry`, "x,y,w,h" in logical px) and only restores a position that is still reachable on a connected screen; otherwise it appears in the bottom-right corner. Its own placement is ignored by the move/resize listeners for 500 ms so it never saves its own echo.
 
-Main close is intercepted and converted to hide. `widget-open-doc` and `widget-open-workspace` are emitted only to `main`; main validates the canonical workspace or existing document, updates normal stores, then shows/unminimizes/focuses its window. The widget hides after handoff so two editors do not race full-document saves; the tray can show it again.
+Drafts: the Quick note box keeps an unsent draft in local storage, so hiding or quitting never loses typed text; a failed save keeps the text.
 
-## Capability boundary
+## Permissions
 
-`capabilities/default.json` includes both `main` and `widget` for core IPC and explicit window operations. `main-window.json` receives the full application command set plus updater/process controls and the autostart permission. `widget-window.json` receives the `widget` set: every registered workspace command granted to `main` except the credential and whole-vault administration commands. A narrower set fails silently: the workspace mounts, then its actions are denied by the window ACL. Twelve commands are withheld — `secret_get`/`secret_set`, `backup_{create,list,restore,delete,prune}`, `vault_rename_{preview,execute}`, `autostart_launch`, `provider_probe`, `sidecar_python_probe` — because they expose credentials or whole-vault administration. The companion holds no secrets (`isWidgetRoute()` strips the API key and skips the keychain entirely) and exposes no Settings surface, so those grants would add access without a feature. Updater and process-exit remain main-window only. `widget-invariants.mjs` asserts this boundary against the registered command set.
+`permissions/widget.toml` is an exact allowlist: `app_boot_ready`, `log_get_or_create`, `doc_save`, `reader_get_bookshelf`. Nothing else: no AI, voice, memory, search, file-system, credential or vault commands. `tests/widget-invariants.mjs` asserts the set exactly, in both directions: a missing command fails at runtime, an extra one widens the surface for no feature.
 
-Write still routes through the dedicated `widget_doc_get`, `widget_doc_save`, and `widget_atomic_save` commands under companion mode. The widget never receives secret read/write permission and has no Settings surface or keychain hydration. Its document list uses `doc_search_full` before storing any document state.
+## Handoff to the main app
 
-## Verification status
+The widget emits `widget-open-workspace` to `main` (`logs` or `reader`). Main validates the workspace id, shows and focuses itself, and the widget hides on `main-window-shown`. Failures come back as `widget-open-failed` and show in the widget bar.
 
